text:
Q: How to copy only path of a folder, without file name, in VS Code? A: <p>Since VS Code doesn't let you pass variables like <code>${fileDirname}</code> directly into the native clipboard command via keybindings, you can use the popular <strong>multi-command</strong> extension to chain a macro.</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Install the <strong>multi-command</strong> extension from the VS Code Marketplace.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Open your <code>keybindings.json</code> file (Press <code>Ctrl+Shift+P</code> or <code>Cmd+Shift+P</code>, type <em>Preferences: Open Keyboard Shortcuts (JSON)</em>).</p>
</li>
<li><p>Add the following keybinding configuration:</p>
<pre><code>{
    &quot;key&quot;: &quot;ctrl+shift+alt+f11&quot;, // Replace with your preferred shortcut
    &quot;command&quot;: &quot;extension.multiCommand.execute&quot;,
    &quot;args&quot;: {
        &quot;sequence&quot;: [
            {
                &quot;command&quot;: &quot;workbench.action.terminal.sendSequence&quot;,
                &quot;args&quot;: {
                    &quot;text&quot;: &quot;echo \&quot;${fileDirname}\&quot; | clip\n&quot; // On Windows (CMD/PowerShell)
                }
            }
        ]
    },
    &quot;when&quot;: &quot;editorTextFocus&quot;
}
</code></pre>
</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Note for Mac/Linux:</strong> If you are on macOS, replace <code>&quot;echo \&quot;${fileDirname}\&quot; | clip\n&quot;</code> with <code>&quot;echo \&quot;${fileDirname}\&quot; | pbcopy\n&quot;</code>. For Linux, use <code>xclip -selection clipboard</code>.</p>
